// The two halves of bare-identifier pattern resolution, each seen through the diagnostic it now
// produces. A bare name is the scrutinee's own payload-free variant when one of that name exists,
// and a plain binding otherwise — so the *same* spelling means different things against different
// scrutinees, and each meaning is checkable.
//
// `lone`   — `String` IS a `Type` case, so it covers that case and nothing else: the `match` is
//            non-exhaustive (E0011), naming exactly the cases left over. Under the old always-binds
//            rule this arm matched every value and the report was E0067 — retired, because what it
//            reported is now the meaning.
// `stray`  — `Missing` is not a `Type` case, so it stays a binding: irrefutable, and the arm below
//            it is dead (E0066). Resolution never widens to names the scrutinee's enum lacks.
// `opaque` — resolution is scrutinee-directed. A `dyn` value has no enum to resolve against, so
//            even `Bool`, which IS a `Type` case, is a plain binding here and closes the match.
// expect: error E0011 at 26:12
// expect: error E0066 at 34:9
// expect: error E0066 at 41:9
// expect: exit 1
enum Type {
    String;
    Int;
    Bool;
    List(inner: string);
}

fn lone(ty: Type): string {
    return match ty {
        String => "string",
    }
}

fn stray(ty: Type): string {
    return match ty {
        Missing => "?",
        Type.Int => "int",
    }
}

fn opaque(d: dyn): string {
    return match d {
        Bool => "bool",
        Type.Int => "int",
    }
}

echo lone(Type.String)
echo stray(Type.Int)
echo opaque(Type.Bool)
